### Why does process isolation matter?

Each instance is a distinct OS process with its own JVM and heap. A field, a static map, a Spring singleton's state, or an in-process cache like Caffeine all live only in that instance's heap. Another instance has its own separate copy and never sees changes. This is why keeping mutable shared state in instance memory is a classic scaling bug: it works in development with one instance and silently breaks in production behind a load balancer.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

// WRONG: local cache that does not sync across instances
public class LocalBalanceCache {

    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, BigDecimal> cache
        = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public void update(String accountId, BigDecimal balance) {
        cache.put(accountId, balance); // Only this instance sees it!
    }

    public BigDecimal get(String accountId) {
        return cache.get(accountId); // Stale on other instances!
    }
}
```

```text
The bug:
  Instance 1: updates Rajesh's balance to 950000 in local cache
  Instance 2: still has 1000000 in its local cache
  Load balancer sends next request to Instance 2
  Result: Rajesh sees wrong (stale) balance!
  Works in dev (1 instance), breaks in prod (N instances)
```

### How does a shared database solve this?

Every instance writes the authoritative value to a shared database and reads it back. This is the correct default for anything that must be consistent and durable, like money.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class AccountService {

    private final AccountRepository repository;

    public AccountService(AccountRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void updateBalance(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Writes to shared database: all instances see it
        repository.updateBalance(accountId, amount);
    }

    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        // Reads from shared database: always current
        return repository.findBalanceByAccountId(accountId);
    }
}
```

```text
Shared database flow:
  Instance 1: UPDATE accounts SET balance=950000 WHERE id='SAV2001'
  Instance 2: SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id='SAV2001'
  Result: Instance 2 reads 950000 (correct, current)
  Cost: database round trip on every read
```

### How does Redis provide fast cross-instance visibility?

Redis is an external, shared, in-memory store that all instances talk to. A value written by App1 is immediately readable by App2. It is used both as a fast cache in front of the database and for shared transient state like sessions and rate-limit counters.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import org.springframework.data.redis.core.StringRedisTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Duration;

@Service
public class RedisBalanceCache {

    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;
    private final AccountRepository repository;

    public RedisBalanceCache(StringRedisTemplate redis,
                              AccountRepository repository) {
        this.redis = redis;
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    // Cache-aside pattern: check Redis, then DB
    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        String cached = redis.opsForValue()
            .get("balance:" + accountId);
        if (cached != null) {
            return new BigDecimal(cached); // Shared cache hit
        }
        // Miss: read from DB, populate Redis
        BigDecimal balance = repository.findBalance(accountId);
        redis.opsForValue().set("balance:" + accountId,
            balance.toPlainString(), Duration.ofSeconds(30));
        return balance;
    }

    public void invalidate(String accountId) {
        redis.delete("balance:" + accountId);
    }
}
```

```text
Redis flow:
  Instance 1: writes balance to DB + invalidates Redis key
  Instance 2: reads from Redis (miss) -> reads from DB -> populates Redis
  Instance 3: reads from Redis (hit) -> returns cached value
  All instances see the same value via shared Redis
```

### How do Kafka events propagate changes?

When App1 makes a change, it publishes an event. All other instances consume it and react by updating their local state or invalidating their local caches. This is push instead of pull: App1 tells everyone when it changes, rather than everyone polling.

```java
package com.bank.events;

import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class BalanceEventPublisher {

    private final KafkaTemplate<String, BalanceChangedEvent> kafka;

    public BalanceEventPublisher(
            KafkaTemplate<String, BalanceChangedEvent> kafka) {
        this.kafka = kafka;
    }

    public void publishBalanceChanged(String accountId,
                                       BigDecimal newBalance) {
        kafka.send("balance-events",
            accountId,
            new BalanceChangedEvent(accountId, newBalance));
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.events;

import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class BalanceEventConsumer {

    private final RedisBalanceCache cache;

    public BalanceEventConsumer(RedisBalanceCache cache) {
        this.cache = cache;
    }

    @KafkaListener(topics = "balance-events")
    public void onBalanceChanged(BalanceChangedEvent event) {
        // Invalidate local and Redis cache so next read is fresh
        cache.invalidate(event.getAccountId());
    }
}
```

```text
Kafka event flow:
  Instance 1: updates DB -> publishes BalanceChangedEvent
  Instance 2: consumes event -> invalidates Redis cache
  Instance 3: consumes event -> invalidates Redis cache
  Next read on any instance: cache miss -> reads fresh from DB
  Trade-off: brief eventual consistency delay between publish and consume
```

### How do the three mechanisms compare?

| Mechanism | Consistency | Latency | Durability | Best For |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Shared DB | Strong | High (disk) | Yes | Source of truth, money |
| Redis | Eventual (TTL) | Low (memory) | No (cache) | Hot reads, sessions |
| Kafka event | Eventual (async) | Medium | Yes (log) | Cache invalidation, reactions |

### How do you layer them in practice?

Combine all three: the database as source of truth, Redis for fast reads, and Kafka events for cache invalidation.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class LayeredAccountService {

    private final AccountRepository repository;
    private final RedisBalanceCache cache;
    private final BalanceEventPublisher publisher;

    public LayeredAccountService(AccountRepository repository,
                                  RedisBalanceCache cache,
                                  BalanceEventPublisher publisher) {
        this.repository = repository;
        this.cache = cache;
        this.publisher = publisher;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void updateBalance(String accountId, BigDecimal newBalance) {
        // 1. Write to DB (source of truth)
        repository.updateBalance(accountId, newBalance);
        // 2. Invalidate Redis (so no stale cache)
        cache.invalidate(accountId);
        // 3. Publish event (so other instances invalidate too)
        publisher.publishBalanceChanged(accountId, newBalance);
    }

    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        // Read path: Redis -> DB (cache-aside)
        return cache.getBalance(accountId);
    }
}
```

```text
Layered approach:
  Write path: DB (truth) -> invalidate Redis -> publish event
  Read path:  Redis (fast) -> DB (on miss)
  Other instances: consume event -> invalidate their Redis keys
  Result: all instances converge quickly to the correct value
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is storing mutable shared state in a local ConcurrentHashMap or Caffeine cache and assuming all instances see updates.

```text
WRONG: local in-memory cache
  Instance 1 updates local cache -> other instances stale
  Works in dev (1 instance), breaks in prod (N instances)

CORRECT: externalized state
  DB for truth + Redis for speed + Kafka events for freshness
  All instances see the same data through shared external stores
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer decides per value whether strong consistency (always read the database) or eventual consistency (cache plus events with a brief stale window) is acceptable. For Rajesh's authoritative balance, the database is the source of truth and every write must go there. For a dashboard counter or a leaderboard, eventual consistency via Redis and events is fine. The layered approach (DB, Redis, Kafka) is not one-size-fits-all; each value has a freshness requirement.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

public class ConsistencyDecision {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Senior decisions per data type:");
        System.out.println("  Balance (critical): always read DB, never local cache");
        System.out.println("  Rate display (non-critical): Redis with 30s TTL is fine");
        System.out.println("  Session token: Redis is the source of truth");
        System.out.println("  Notification badge count: eventual via Kafka events");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Never store mutable shared state in local JVM memory.");
        System.out.println("Local caches are read-only and must be invalidated.");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Senior decisions per data type:
  Balance (critical): always read DB, never local cache
  Rate display (non-critical): Redis with 30s TTL is fine
  Session token: Redis is the source of truth
  Notification badge count: eventual via Kafka events

Never store mutable shared state in local JVM memory.
Local caches are read-only and must be invalidated.
```
